<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Galicia-Volhynia</h1><p class="st-meta">1199 CE – 1349 CE · capital Halych</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The leading western Rus' state after the Mongol invasion, whose ruler Daniel was crowned King of Rus' and founded the city of Lviv.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1199 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-the-great">Roman Mstislavich</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-volhynia">Principality of Volhynia</a> took <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-galicia">Halych</a> and united it with his own lands. After his death in battle in 1205 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-galicia-and-lodomeria">Galicia</a> fell into decades of strife involving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. His son Daniel won back both lands by 1238 and made the state one of the most powerful in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Daniel submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan">Batu Khan</a> in 1245 but looked to the West for help, and in 1253 he was crowned King of Rus' by a papal legate at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drohiczyn">Dorohychyn</a>. He founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelm">Kholm</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lviv</a>, which he named after his son Leo, and welcomed German, Armenian and Jewish settlers to the towns. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a>, Catholic and steppe influences met in its court.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The direct dynasty ended in 1323, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuri-ii-boleslav">Yuri II Boleslaw</a>, a Masovian Piast, was poisoned by boyars in 1340. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Lithuania</a> then divided the kingdom, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iii-the-great">Casimir III</a> taking <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-galicia-and-lodomeria">Galicia</a> by 1349 and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-volhynia">Duchy of Volhynia</a> going to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-galicia-volhynia.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Galicia-Volhynia" width="320" height="266"><figcaption>Image: Hkzonda · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Halych-Volhynia_general.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1199 CE – 1349 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Halych</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Roman Mstislavich, Daniel of Galicia, Leo I</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old East Slavic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Ukraine, Poland, Belarus</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Ruthenia, Principality of Galicia-Volhynia, Galicia-Volhynia</dd></dl></aside></div>
